1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,338
Chapter one. Twelve cameras.

2
00:00:02,688 --> 00:00:12,557
The experiment was funded by Leland Stanford, a railroad millionaire and horse breeder. Legend says there was a bet involved, though no one could ever prove it.

3
00:00:12,757 --> 00:00:19,857
The photographer was named Eadweard Muybridge. Cameras back then were terribly slow, and it took him years to pull it off.

4
00:00:20,057 --> 00:00:27,514
In 1878, in front of journalists, a mare named Sallie Gardner ran past the twelve cameras.

5
00:00:27,714 --> 00:00:35,082
In some photos, all four legs were in the air. But tucked beneath her belly, not stretched out like in the paintings.

6
00:00:35,282 --> 00:00:43,766
Muybridge did something else: he placed the photos on a wheel and projected them one after another. The horse ran again, now on a wall.

7
00:00:44,216 --> 00:00:49,083
Why do still photos look like a running horse? Because the brain fills in the gaps.

8
00:00:49,283 --> 00:00:57,634
If you see more than ten or twelve images per second, you no longer see photos: you see motion. Just like a flipbook with drawings in the corners.

9
00:00:58,534 --> 00:01:02,731
There was motion now. What was missing was showing it to a full room.
